Let $\tau\colon(0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable. We call $\tau$ a \textit{quadruple transformation} if there is a constant  $\ctrano{\tau}\in[0, \infty)$ such that the following condition holds:
For every metric space $(\mc Q, d)$ and pairwise distinct points $y,z,q,p\in\mc Q$ such that
\begin{equation*}
	\ol yq^2 - \ol yp^2 - \ol zq^2 + \ol zp^2 \leq 2\, \ol qp\, \ol yz\, ,
\end{equation*}
we also have
\begin{equation*}
	\tau(\ol yq)  - \tau(\ol yp) - \tau(\ol zq) + \tau(\ol zp) \leq \ctrano{\tau}\,  \ol qp \, \tau^\prime(\ol yz)
	\, .
\end{equation*}
Define the \textit{quadruple constant} of a quadruple transformation $\tau$ as the smallest possible choice of $\ctrano{\tau}$ and denote it as $\ctranobest{\tau}$.
Let $\mc{T}$ denote the \textit{set of all quadruple transformations}.

